            <DIV class=3Dsection>
            <H2 class=3DsubTitle id=3DtocHeadRef>SUMMARY</H2>
            <SCRIPT type=3Dtext/javascript>loadTOCNode(1, =
'summary');</SCRIPT>

            <DIV class=3Dsbody>VBRun60sp6.exe is a self-extracting =
executable file=20
            that installs the latest versions of the Microsoft Visual =
Basic=20
            run-time files that are required by all applications that =
are=20
            created with Visual Basic 6.0. The files include the fixes =
that are=20
            included with Visual Studio 6.0 Service Pack 6.=20
            <P class=3DtopOfPage><A=20
            href=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/kb/290887#top"><IMG =
alt=3D""=20
            =
src=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/library/images/support/kbgraphics/pub=
lic/en-us/uparrow.gif">Back=20
            to the top</A></P></DIV>
            <H2 class=3DsubTitle id=3DtocHeadRef>MORE INFORMATION</H2>
            <SCRIPT=20
            type=3Dtext/javascript>loadTOCNode(1, =
'moreinformation');</SCRIPT>

            <DIV class=3Dsbody>To obtain the VBRun60sp6.exe file, visit =
the=20
            following Microsoft Web site:
            <DIV class=3Dindent><SPAN class=3Dll><A=20
            =
href=3D"http://www.microsoft.com/downloads/details.aspx?FamilyId=3D7B9BA2=
61-7A9C-43E7-9117-F673077FFB3C">http://www.microsoft.com/downloads/detail=
s.aspx?FamilyId=3D7B9BA261-7A9C-43E7-9117-F673077FFB3C</A></SPAN><SPAN=20
            class=3DpLink>=20
            =
(http://www.microsoft.com/downloads/details.aspx?FamilyId=3D7B9BA261-7A9C=
-43E7-9117-F673077FFB3C)</SPAN></DIV>Release=20
            Date: March 29, 2004<BR><BR>For additional information about =
how to=20
            download Microsoft Support files, click the following =
article number=20
            to view the article in the Microsoft Knowledge Base:=20
            <DIV class=3Dindent><A class=3DKBlink=20
            =
href=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/kb/119591/">119591</A><SPAN=20
            class=3DpLink> =
(http://support.microsoft.com/kb/119591/)</SPAN> How to=20
            Obtain Microsoft Support Files from Online Services =
</DIV>Microsoft=20
            scanned this file for viruses. Microsoft used the most =
current=20
            virus-detection software that was available on the date that =
the=20
            file was posted. The file is stored on security-enhanced =
servers=20
            that help to prevent any unauthorized changes to the file.=20
            VBRun60sp6.exe installs the following core files that are =
included=20
            with Visual Studio 6.0 Service Pack 6. These files are the =
base=20
            dependencies for any component or application that is =
created in=20
            Visual Basic 6.0:<BR><BR>
            <TABLE class=3Dtable cellSpacing=3D1>
              <TBODY>
              <TR>
                <TH><B>File name</B></TH>
                <TH><B>Version</B></TH>
                <TH><B>Size</B></TH></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Asycfilt.dll</TD>
                <TD>2.40.4275.1</TD>
                <TD>144 KB (147,728 bytes)</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Comcat.dll</TD>
                <TD>4.71.1460.1</TD>
                <TD>21.7 KB (22,288 bytes)</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Msvbvm60.dll</TD>
                <TD>6.0.97.82</TD>
                <TD>1.32 MB (1,386,496 bytes)</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Oleaut32.dll</TD>
                <TD>2.40.4275.1</TD>
                <TD>584 KB (598,288 bytes)</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Olepro32.dll</TD>
                <TD>5.0.4275.1</TD>
                <TD>160 KB (164,112 bytes)</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Stdole2.tlb</TD>
                <TD>2.40.4275.1</TD>
                <TD>17.5 KB (17,920=20
            bytes)</TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><BR>VBRun60sp6.exe also =
installs the=20
            following files. These files are supporting files for the=20
            VBRun60sp6.exe self-extracting file: <BR><BR>
            <TABLE class=3Dtable cellSpacing=3D1>
              <TBODY>
              <TR>
                <TH><B>File name</B></TH>
                <TH><B>Version</B></TH>
                <TH><B>Size</B></TH></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Advpack.dll</TD>
                <TD>4.71.1015.0</TD>
                <TD>73.2 KB (74,960 bytes)</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Vbrun60.inf</TD>
                <TD>Not Applicable</TD>
                <TD>1.04 KB (1,069 bytes)</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>W95inf16.dll</TD>
                <TD>4.71.704.0</TD>
                <TD>2.21 KB (2,272 bytes)</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>W95inf32.dll</TD>
                <TD>4.71.16.0</TD>
                <TD>4.5 KB (4,608 =
bytes)</TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><BR>For=20
            information about the command-line arguments that are =
available for=20
            VBRun60sp6.exe, type the following at a command line:=20
            <DIV class=3Dindent><SPAN class=3DuserInput>VBRun60sp6.exe=20
            /?</SPAN></DIV><B>Note</B> You may have to specify the =
complete path=20
            of the VBRun60sp6.exe file in the command.<BR><BR>For =
additional=20
            information about command-line arguments for this file, =
click the=20
            following article number to view the article in the =
Microsoft=20
            Knowledge Base:=20
            <DIV class=3Dindent><A class=3DKBlink=20
            =
href=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/kb/189520/">189520</A><SPAN=20
            class=3DpLink> =
(http://support.microsoft.com/kb/189520/)</SPAN>=20
            Outlook Deployment Kit (ODK) Setup command-line options =
</DIV>
            <P class=3DtopOfPage><A=20
            href=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/kb/290887#top"><IMG =
alt=3D""=20
            =
src=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/library/images/support/kbgraphics/pub=
lic/en-us/uparrow.gif">Back=20
            to the top</A></P>
            <H3 id=3DtocHeadRef>Do I need the VBRun60sp6.exe file?</H3>
            <SCRIPT=20
            type=3Dtext/javascript>loadTOCNode(2, =
'moreinformation');</SCRIPT>
            The VBRun60sp6.exe file is not intended to replace the =
Package and=20
            Deployment Wizard (PDW) for distributing Visual Basic =
applications.=20
            For example, if your application includes components such as =
ActiveX=20
            controls or DLLs, use the PDW or a third-party setup package =
for=20
            distribution. However, if your Visual Basic application =
depends on=20
            only the files that are included in the VBRun60sp6.exe file, =
you can=20
            distribute your application by providing the executable =
(.exe) file=20
            and VBRun60sp6.exe to end users.<BR><BR>To determine whether =
your=20
            application requires additional files for distribution, you =
can use=20
            the PDW to create a set of setup files as a test. When the =
PDW=20
            creates a setup package, the PDW also creates a Setup.lst =
file. Open=20
            the Setup.lst file in a text editor such as Notepad. If the =
[Setup1=20
            Files] section of the Setup.lst file contains only your .exe =
file,=20
            end users should be able to run your application after they =
run the=20
            VBRun60sp6.exe file to install the core run-time files. =
However, if=20
            the [Setup1 Files] section contains multiple files, consider =
using=20
            the PDW or a third-party setup package for =
distribution.<BR><BR>In=20
            addition to distributing simple executables, you can also =
use the=20
            VBRun60sp6.exe file for the following:=20
            <TABLE class=3D"list ul">
              <TBODY>
              <TR>
                <TD class=3Dbullet>=E2=80=A2</TD>
                <TD class=3Dtext>To minimize the size of Internet =
downloads of=20
                  Visual Basic applications.<BR><BR>By running the=20
                  Vbrun60sp6.exe file ahead of time, users can download =
your=20
                  application from the Web faster.</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD class=3Dbullet>=E2=80=A2</TD>
                <TD class=3Dtext>As a troubleshooting step when an =
installation=20
                  of Visual Basic or a Visual Basic application =
fails.<BR><BR>If=20
                  Setup fails with an error message that mentions one of =
the=20
                  core files, or if registration of a component fails =
during=20
                  Setup, the core files on the target computer might be=20
                  mismatched. If the versions of the files in the =
VBRun60sp6.exe=20
                  file are newer than the versions on the target =
computer, you=20
                  might resolve the problem by running the =
VBRun60sp6.exe file=20
                  before you run Setup.</TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>For =
additional=20
            information about a specific example of a Setup problem that =
is=20
            resolved by running VBRun60.exe or VBRun60sp6.exe, click the =

            following article number to view the article in the =
Microsoft=20
            Knowledge Base:=20
            <DIV class=3Dindent><A class=3DKBlink=20
            =
href=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/kb/194754/">194754</A><SPAN=20
            class=3DpLink> =
(http://support.microsoft.com/kb/194754/)</SPAN>=20
            Setup.exe file linked to missing export Oleaut32.dll:185 =
</DIV>You=20
            can obtain a self-extracting executable file that installs =
the=20
            original Visual Basic 6.0 run-time files by extracting =
VBRun60.exe=20
            from the VBRun60.cab file. The VBRun60.cab file is available =
on the=20
            Visual Basic and Visual Studio CDs. The VBRun60.cab file is =
located=20
            in the \Common\Tools\Vb\Cabinets folder on the Visual Basic =
6.0 CD=20
            (Disk 1).<BR><BR>On the Visual Studio 6.0 Professional CDs, =
the=20
            VBRun60.cab file is located in the same relative path on =
Disk 2. On=20
            the Visual Studio 6.0 Enterprise CDs, it is located on Disk=20
            3.<BR><BR><B>Note</B> The version of the VBRun60sp6.exe file =
that is=20
            included in this Knowledge Base article is different from =
the one on=20
            the installation CD. The version that is included in this =
article is=20
            the latest version. This version is included with Visual =
Studio 6.0=20
            Service Pack 6.<BR><BR>You can use the Extract utility to =
extract=20
            the VBRun60.exe file from the VBRun60.cab cabinet file. The=20
            Extract.exe file is located in your Windows folder or on =
your=20
            installation CD for Microsoft Windows 95, Windows 98, or =
Windows NT.=20
            At an MS-DOS command prompt, run the following command to =
extract=20
            the file in the cabinet file into the current directory:=20
            <DIV class=3Dindent><SPAN class=3DuserInput>Extract=20
            Vbrun60.cab</SPAN></DIV>For additional information about =
using the=20
            Extract utility, click the following article number to view =
the=20
            article in the Microsoft Knowledge Base:=20
            <DIV class=3Dindent><A class=3DKBlink=20
            =
href=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/kb/129605/">129605</A><SPAN=20
            class=3DpLink> =
(http://support.microsoft.com/kb/129605/)</SPAN> How to=20
            extract original compressed Windows files </DIV>
